  "Name": "Kats2016-Ag-aSi-Ag-no-top-aSi",
  "Source": "M. A. Kats and F. Capasso, \"Optical absorbers based on strong interference in ultra-thin films,\" Laser Photonics Rev., 1-15 (2016) (Early View; volume and final pages not printed in this copy). doi:10.1002/lpor.201600098 - Fig. 5(a)-(b), p. 8: structure of Fig. 5(a) without the top 12 nm a-Si",
  "Notes": "Same absorber without the top a-Si layer (single Ag/a-Si/Ag cavity), the comparison case of Fig. 5(b)\nSpecification: Reference case (narrow-band absorption) for Fig. 5(b).\nReported: Fig. 5(b) blue curves (read off the plot, approximate): narrow absorption peak ~60-65 % near 555 nm, ~15 % at 400-480 nm, ~5 % beyond 650 nm.\nNotes: Same assumptions as Kats2016-aSi-Ag-aSi-Ag-broadband (glass n = 1.52, library a-Si and Ag, normal incidence).\nLayers from Fig. 5(a)-(b), p. 8: structure of Fig. 5(a) without the top 12 nm a-Si; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours A peak 33.8 % at 545 nm; paper A peak ~60-65 % near 555 nm (read off Fig. 5b). The paper does not give its a-Si and Ag data; with Pierce & Spicer a-Si and Johnson & Christy Ag the peak is about half the plotted one, so the paper’s a-Si is probably more absorbing.",
